module sysl.posix.tty
@requires(posix)

import sysl.text.cstring

// Getting the kernel out of the way, so a program can read a terminal a keystroke at a time.
//
// **This is what makes one program run at a macOS terminal and over a serial cable to a board.** At a
// shell the line discipline echoes what is typed, honours backspace, and releases a whole line at
// Enter -- so `sysl.term.edit` would echo a second copy of every character and never see a keystroke
// until it was too late to act on one. Over a USB CDC port there is no line discipline to begin with,
// which is why the editor exists. These two functions are the difference between those situations,
// and they are the *only* difference: with them the same program serves both.
//
// A program that wants whole lines and no editing wants none of this. `sysl.io.console_lines` reads a
// cooked terminal, the kernel does the work, and that is the right facility for anything that is not
// drawing its own line.
//
// ## It is `stty`, and that is a decision rather than an expedient
//
// The C way is `tcgetattr` / `cfmakeraw` / `tcsetattr`, and `struct termios` is caller-allocated --
// so a binding needs either its size, which only the header knows, or a C shim to hold it. **The
// standard library can have neither.** `sysl.fs` states the constraint the whole of `library/sysl` is
// written under -- everything is reached by symbol alone, no header included and no C structure
// transcribed -- and it is why `stat` and `readdir` are absent there rather than merely unwritten.
//
// A shim would break something larger: the library works the same for every target *because* it is
// one tree of sysl that every target compiles, and library C is compiled by sysl for the target, so a
// `.c` here would need a C compiler and a sysroot on every target that did not prune it. That is a
// guarantee traded for a convenience, and it is not a good trade for two flags.
//
// `system("stty …")` is one extern of `*u8 -> int`, reached by symbol alone, transcribing nothing.
// The cost is a shell spawn, which for something called twice at the edges of a session is nothing.
//
// ## What it actually sets: `-icanon -echo -isig`, and what it deliberately leaves alone
//
// **Output translation is asserted rather than assumed**, which is the one thing full raw mode takes
// that is worth keeping. `opost onlcr` says `\n` becomes `\r\n` on the way out, so every `print` in
// the program goes on working; without it a hosted program's output walks diagonally down the screen,
// each line starting where the last one ended, and the fix for *that* is auditing every write in the
// program rather than this one call.
//
// **Naming them is not redundant, and this cost a session.** Nothing here turns output processing
// off, so leaving the two out looked safe — and it is, on a terminal that still has them. A terminal
// does **not** always still have them: a program killed before it could restore, a `stty` run
// earlier, an emulator's own defaults. Under a fresh pty the omission is invisible, which is exactly
// why the tests were green while a real terminal stair-stepped. A mode this sets should not depend on
// the mode it found.
//
// `sysl.term.edit` writes `\r\n` outright, which a board needs and which costs a redundant carriage
// return here — the cursor is moved to a column it is already in. That is also why the editor's own
// output looked right while everything printed around it did not.
//
// **Signals go, and that is not the obvious choice.** Leaving `isig` alone would keep Ctrl-C
// interrupting, which reads like a feature for a REPL. It cannot be had: a program interrupted in
// cbreak mode must restore the terminal from a signal handler, and restoring means allocating a
// command string and forking a shell, neither of which is async-signal-safe. Tried, and it did not
// merely risk a deadlock — it hung every time.
//
// So `-isig`, and Ctrl-C arrives as **byte 3** for `sysl.term.edit` to decide about. What that costs
// is real and should be said plainly: a program that has stopped responding can no longer be
// interrupted from its own terminal, and the escape is `kill` from another one. What it buys is that
// the terminal is never left broken, and that **a Mac behaves exactly like the board** — which has no
// signals to disable in the first place.

// `system` runs a command through the shell and answers its exit status; `atexit` runs a function on
// the way out, which is what a *process* needs here rather than a destructor: the terminal's mode is
// the process's, not a value's, so there is no value whose death would restore it (`03 § A
// destructor` also does not run for module storage at exit).
//
// **There is no signal handler here, and that is a decision this file paid for once.** `atexit` runs
// on `exit` and not when a signal terminates a process, so an earlier version of this installed
// handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM that restored the terminal and exited. It **hung**, reliably, and
// the reason is worth writing down: restoring means `cooked`, which means `stty`, which builds a
// command string -- an **allocation** -- and hands it to `system`, which **forks**. Neither is
// async-signal-safe. A handler that allocates while the interrupted code holds the allocator's lock
// waits for a lock only the interrupted code can release, and a program that would merely have left
// the terminal in a bad state instead stops responding entirely.
//
// **Without `termios` there is no way to restore a terminal from a handler at all**, because every
// route to it allocates or forks. So the signal is removed instead of handled: `-isig` below means
// Ctrl-C never becomes one, and every exit is then an ordinary exit that `atexit` really does catch.
//
// Declared here rather than in `sysl.sys`, following `sysl.fs` and the file beside this one:
// `sysl.sys` is the printing-and-reading core, carries no capability, and a POSIX-only symbol in it
// would be a requirement filed where nothing declares one.
private[sysl] extern "system" c_system(cmd: *u8) -> int
private[sysl] extern "atexit" c_atexit(f: *extern() -> unit) -> int
private[sysl] extern "fflush" c_fflush(stream: *u8) -> int

// The descriptor a terminal's input arrives on, named beside the two the file already names.
private stdin_fd() -> int = 0

// Whether this program has put the terminal into cbreak mode and not yet put it back.
//
// **It is module storage because the terminal is**: there is one of it per process, and a second
// `raw()` while the first is in force is a program asking for something it already has rather than a
// second thing to undo. It is also what keeps the exit hook honest -- a program that never called
// `raw` must not have its terminal "restored" to settings it never chose.
private var entered: bool = false

// Whether the exit hook has been registered, so that repeated `raw()` calls register one hook rather
// than one per call.
private var hooked: bool = false

// One `stty` invocation, answering whether it worked.
//
// It names no device. `stty` acts on **its own standard input**, which through `system` is the
// shell's, which is this program's -- so there is no `-f` (BSD) against `-F` (GNU) divergence to
// handle, and one command string serves both platforms. A program whose input is redirected gets a
// failure here rather than a silent no-op, which is what `raw` reports on.
private stty(args: string) -> bool
    val c = cstring("stty " + args)

    c_system(c.ptr) == 0
end stty

// Put the terminal into cbreak mode: keystrokes arrive as they are typed and nothing is echoed.
//
// **Answers whether it worked**, and a caller should look. It fails where there is no terminal to
// change -- input redirected from a file or a pipe -- and that is not an error so much as a different
// situation, in which `sysl.io.console_lines` is the facility that fits and no mode has to change.
//
//     if raw()
//         var ed = editor(&input, &output)
//         for line in ed do ...
//         cooked()
//
// `min 1 time 0` is what makes a read block until at least one byte arrives instead of returning
// immediately with none; without `icanon` the terminal has no other rule about when to answer, and a
// reader spinning on empty reads is the shape that gets mistaken for a hung program.
//
// Calling it twice is not an error and does nothing the second time.
raw() -> bool
    if entered then return true
    if !is_tty(stdin_fd()) then return false
    if !stty("-icanon -echo -isig opost onlcr min 1 time 0") then return false

    entered = true

    // **The hook is why a terminal is not left wrecked by an exit nobody wrote.** What has to be
    // undone belongs to the process rather than to a value, so no destructor could hang on it -- and
    // a program that ended with echo off leaves a shell showing nothing as it is typed, which the
    // user has to fix by typing `stty sane` blind.
    //
    // One hook is enough **because `-isig` removed the exits it could not have caught.** With signals
    // off, Ctrl-C is a byte the editor reads rather than a death the program never hears about, so
    // every way out of a program in cbreak mode is now an ordinary `exit` -- which is exactly what
    // `atexit` covers.
    //
    // Registered once, on first entry, and `cooked` is a no-op when the program restored the terminal
    // itself, so nothing here fires twice.
    if !hooked
        c_atexit(&restore)
        hooked = true

    true
end raw

// Put back what `raw` changed, and only what it changed.
//
// **`icanon echo isig` rather than `stty sane`**, which would also reset everything else the terminal
// was set to -- settings this program did not choose and has no business having an opinion about.
// Doing nothing when `raw` was never called or has already been undone is part of the same rule.
cooked()
    if !entered then return

    stty("icanon echo isig")
    entered = false
end cooked

// What `atexit` runs. It is `cooked` under another name, because `atexit` wants a symbol and the
// exit path wants exactly the ordinary restore.
private restore()
    cooked()

// ## Getting output out, which a terminal needs and a pipe does not
//
// **`print` does not reach the screen when you expect it to, and that breaks every prompt.**
// `sysl.putbytes` -- the one sink all printing goes through -- writes with C's `putchar`, and C
// line-buffers a stream attached to a terminal. So a prompt written without a newline sits in the
// buffer, and so does every character an editor echoes, until something writes a `\n`. What a person
// sees is a program that prints nothing while they type and then produces the whole line at once,
// which reads as an editor that is not echoing at all.
//
// A board never shows this, which is why it was found here and not there: its `putchar` is the SDK's
// and has no buffer in front of it.
//
// **The exact fix would be `setvbuf(stdout, …)` once, and it cannot be written.** `setvbuf` needs
// C's `stdout`, which is a global *variable*, and sysl has no `extern` form for one -- `15 §7` names
// a layout, a `#define` and a by-value struct as the unreachables and does not mention this, so it is
// a gap rather than a decision. `fflush(null)` is the way around it: it flushes every stream and so
// needs to name none.

// Push out everything the C library is holding, on every stream.
//
// **A caller needs this for its own prompt.** The editor's sink below flushes what the *editor*
// writes; a prompt printed by the program before handing over is the program's own output, and
// nothing has written a newline after it. `prints("ogol> ")` then `flush()` is the whole idiom.
flush() = c_fflush(null)

// Standard output as a sink that **flushes what it is given**, which is what an editor must write
// through on a hosted terminal.
//
// It carries no state for the reason `Stdout` does not: a destination fixed at compile time keeps
// none, so the pointer the sink is built on addresses nothing and `write` never looks at `self`.
//
// A program on a board passes its own port's writer and needs none of this. That asymmetry is real
// and is the only one in the whole facility: the board has no buffer to defeat.
struct TtyWriter
end TtyWriter

impl Fallible for TtyWriter

impl Writer for TtyWriter
    write(*self, bytes: []const u8)
        putbytes(bytes)
        c_fflush(null)
end TtyWriter

val tty_sink: *TtyWriter = ptr_cast(0usize)

tty_writer() -> *Writer = tty_sink
